{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Multimedia Storyboarding and AI Asset Generation","title":"Narration and Modality Decisions","description":"AIL 606 textbook section connecting narration and modality decisions to storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next."],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_multimedia_storyboarding_and_ai_asset_generation","ail606_supplement_narration_and_modality_decisions","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":4,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"03","section":"04","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/03/03"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_3_4_narration_and_modality_decisions_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_3_4_narration_and_modality_decisions_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_3_4_narration_and_modality_decisions_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_3_4_narration_and_modality_decisions_calibration"]},"content":"# Narration and Modality Decisions\n\n![Narration and Modality Decisions textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/03-04-narration-and-modality-decisions.png)\n\nThe single most common multimedia mistake is also the most well-intentioned: a designer adds narration that reads the on-screen text aloud, \"so the lesson is multimedia.\" This duplicates the same words in two channels and, by the redundancy principle, usually depresses learning rather than helping it. The modality decision, whether words should arrive as on-screen text or as narration, is not a production preference; it is a load-management decision grounded in the dual-channel model of working memory, and it has conditions, limits, and an accessibility cost that designers routinely ignore.\n\nThis section gives you the actual rule, not the slogan. The modality principle (\"present words as speech rather than on-screen text when they accompany a graphic\") holds only under specific conditions, transient material, system-paced presentation, words paired with a visual the eyes must watch, and it carries an obligation, because audio that helps a sighted, hearing learner excludes a deaf learner unless an equivalent is provided. Knowing when the principle does not apply is as important as knowing when it does.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA designer animates the four phases of mitosis, then adds narration that reads aloud, word for word, the full explanatory paragraph already printed on the frame. She is pleased: it now has motion, text, and voice. But a learner watching this frame must split the visual channel between the animation and the identical printed paragraph, while the ear receives nothing new. The eyes lose the animation to re-read words the ear is already delivering. The \"richer\" frame teaches less than a version with brief labels and audio explanation.\n\nThe frame looks more complete because it has more media, and that appearance is the problem. This section asks you to expose the modality logic the extra voiceover hides: more channels are not better; the right words in the right channel, with an access equivalent, is better.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nThe foundation is the **dual-channel assumption**: working memory processes visual and auditory information through partly separate channels, each with limited capacity. When a graphic must be watched and words must be processed at the same time, putting the words on screen forces both into the visual channel and overloads it; speaking the words instead spreads the load across both channels. That is the **modality principle**.\n\nBut the principle is conditional, and the conditions are where the judgment lives:\n\n- **Transience.** It applies to transient, system-paced material (animation, video). For static text the learner can reread at will, on-screen text is fine, and narration may even hurt because the learner cannot scan back through speech.\n- **Redundancy.** Narration must complement the visual, not duplicate on-screen text. Identical words in both channels (the redundancy principle) overload rather than offload; the fix is brief labels on screen plus distinct spoken explanation.\n- **Complexity and pacing.** For dense or unfamiliar content, even well-placed narration can outrun the learner; segmenting and learner control become the safety valve.\n\nUDL learner variability supplies the constraint the principle ignores: audio that helps a hearing learner excludes deaf and hard-of-hearing learners, and audio-only delivery fails in sound-off contexts. So a modality decision is never finished until it specifies the **access equivalent** (synchronized captions, a transcript). Cognitive load theory frames the whole choice: the goal is to keep extraneous load down (no redundancy) while keeping germane processing reachable across channels.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"State the dual-channel assumption in your own words, then explain why narrating words verbatim from on-screen text overloads a learner rather than helping, even though it 'uses two channels.'\" answer=\"The dual-channel assumption holds that working memory processes visual and auditory information through partly separate, limited-capacity channels. Reading on-screen text aloud verbatim does not split the load across channels: the words are duplicated, so the ear receives nothing new while the eyes must still process the identical printed paragraph alongside the graphic. That is the redundancy principle, and it overloads the visual channel instead of offloading it. Modality helps only when the spoken words replace, not echo, the on-screen text.\"></self-explain>\n\n\n## Worked Example: A Modality Decision on One Process Frame\n\nThe frame teaches the four phases of mitosis using a short animation. Below is the before/after of the narration and modality choice.\n\n**Before (redundant and overloaded):**\n\n> Frame 3. The animation plays. The full explanatory paragraph is printed on screen, and the narrator reads that paragraph word for word. Caption: added narration so it is multimedia.\n\nProblems: on-screen text and narration are identical (redundancy), and the eyes must split between watching the animation and reading the same words. The visual channel is overloaded.\n\n**After (words offloaded to audio, equivalent provided):**\n\n> Frame 3. The animation plays with brief on-screen labels only (phase names). The narration explains the transitions in concise, learner-paced segments, distinct from the labels. A synchronized caption track carries the narration text for access.\n\nAnnotated decision trail:\n\n1. Removed the verbatim paragraph: kept short labels on screen, moved the explanation to narration so the eyes can follow the animation (modality offloads words to audio).\n2. Checked the redundancy limit: narration now complements the visual instead of duplicating printed text.\n3. Scoped the modality claim: this is transient explanatory content paired with a graphic, the case where narration helps; a reference table on a later frame was left as text.\n4. Added an equivalent: captions and a transcript so deaf or hard-of-hearing learners, and those in audio-off settings, are not excluded.\n5. Rejected suggestion: the AI offered to keep the full paragraph and also narrate it for emphasis. Rejected, because that restores the redundancy the revision removed.\n\nThe improvement is a modality decision with a stated limit and an access equivalent, not just an added soundtrack.\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nDecide modality by interrogating the frame, not by habit. For each block of words, ask the three diagnostic questions:\n\n1. **Is the visual transient and must the eyes watch it?** If yes (animation, a process unfolding), speak the words so the eyes stay on the visual. If the visual is static and rereadable, on-screen text is usually fine.\n2. **Are these words already on screen?** If you are about to narrate text the learner can also read, stop, that is redundancy. Reduce the on-screen text to brief labels and let narration carry the distinct explanation.\n3. **What is the access equivalent?** Any narration decision is incomplete until you name the caption track or transcript that delivers the same content without sound.\n\nA defensible modality choice names the condition that triggered it (transient visual, paired graphic) and the equivalent that protects access. If you cannot name both, you have a preference, not a decision.\n\nWalk a mixed frame where one consistent treatment is the wrong instinct.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Two blocks, two opposite modality decisions\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"An onboarding frame has a 30-second checkout animation and a static return-policy table. You want one consistent treatment. Start with the animation: how should its words arrive?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"On-screen paragraph, so learners can reread the steps\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"The animation is transient and the eyes must watch it. On-screen text forces both the motion and the words into the visual channel, overloading it.\"},{\"label\":\"Concise narration with brief on-screen labels\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Correct: the visual is transient and paired, so speaking the words keeps the eyes on the motion. Modality applies here.\"}]},\"n2\":{\"prompt\":\"Learners miss steps while re-reading. What condition did you ignore?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Transience: the eyes must watch the moving visual, so speak the words\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Transient paired visuals are exactly the modality case.\"}],\"feedback\":\"Static-text reasoning does not apply to a moving visual.\"},\"n3\":{\"prompt\":\"Now the static return-policy table. Apply the same narration treatment?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Yes, narrate it too for consistency\",\"next\":\"n4\",\"feedback\":\"Narrating dense, learner-paced reference content forces a linear pace on material learners need to scan and compare. Modality does not apply to static text.\"},{\"label\":\"No, keep it as on-screen text and do not narrate it\",\"next\":\"n5\",\"feedback\":\"Correct: static, rereadable content is the case where text beats narration.\"}]},\"n4\":{\"prompt\":\"Learners cannot compare policy rows at a linear audio pace. What is the rule?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Leave static reference content as text; do not narrate it\",\"next\":\"n5\",\"feedback\":\"Right: modality is decided block by block, not as a global setting.\"}],\"feedback\":\"One consistent treatment was the wrong instinct.\"},\"n5\":{\"prompt\":\"You narrated the animation. What decision is still unfinished?\",\"feedback\":\"Every audio decision pulls an accessibility decision behind it: you must name the synchronized caption track and transcript so deaf, hard-of-hearing, and audio-off learners get the step explanations. A modality choice without an access equivalent is incomplete.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is \"narration is always better than text.\" It is conditional; for static, learner-paced reference material, on-screen text can beat narration, and narrating it adds nothing the learner cannot already control. The second misreading treats captions as optional polish for the modality decision. They are not; once you offload words to audio for load reasons, you have created an access gap, and the captions or transcript are the part of the decision that closes it. A modality choice without an equivalent is an incomplete decision, not a finished one with a nice-to-have missing.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A dense reference table of return-policy rules is static and learner-paced. Should you narrate it under the modality principle?\" explanation=\"No. The modality principle applies to transient, system-paced visuals the eyes must watch. A static, rereadable table is exactly the case where on-screen text wins, because narration would force a linear pace on content learners need to scan and compare. Narrating it adds nothing the learner cannot already control.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Yes, narration is always better than text\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"No, the content is static and learner-paced, so on-screen text serves it better than narration\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Yes, because two channels always reduce load\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nWalk a mixed frame. An onboarding module has two parts: a thirty-second animation of a checkout flow, and a static reference table of return-policy rules. A designer wants one consistent treatment, narrate both. Apply the diagnostics. The animation is transient and the eyes must watch it, so narrate the steps with only short labels on screen (modality applies). The policy table is static, dense, and learner-paced; narrating it would force a linear pace on material learners need to scan and compare, so leave it as on-screen text and do not narrate it (modality does not apply). One frame, two opposite decisions, each tied to the transience condition.\n\nNow close the access gap created by the animation's narration: add a synchronized caption track and a transcript, so a deaf learner gets the step explanations and an audio-off learner is not blocked. The walkthrough shows the principle is not a global setting; it is decided block by block, and every audio decision pulls an accessibility decision behind it.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nRead every \"added narration\" as a hypothesis with conditions, and check the conditions. For each spoken block in the chapter's examples, verify it is transient and paired with a visual, and confirm the on-screen text is not the same words. Then look for the access equivalent and flag its absence. A strong reader notices both failure modes: the redundant voiceover that duplicates text, and the bare audio with no caption. Keep a note of where the chapter narrates and where it deliberately does not, and recover the condition behind each.\n\nA strong reader of modality decisions can, for any block of words, name the channel chosen, the condition that justifies it, the redundancy it avoids, and the equivalent that keeps it accessible.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact reaches the stronger standard when the trace shows a per-block modality decision with its triggering condition and its access equivalent, including at least one place where the designer chose text over narration on purpose. It documents removing a redundancy (cutting an on-screen paragraph that was also narrated) and naming the caption or transcript that accompanies any audio. Where an AI tool offered to \"narrate everything for emphasis,\" the trace records rejecting it for reintroducing redundancy.\n\nA strong closing note reads: \"On the mitosis animation I cut the printed paragraph to short phase labels and moved the explanation to narration, because the eyes must follow transient motion; I added captions and a transcript so the audio is not load-bearing for access. On the later reference table I kept text and added no narration, because that content is static and learner-paced. I rejected the suggestion to keep and also narrate the paragraph, which would restore the redundancy I removed.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the decision to a low-bandwidth or offline mobile context where reliable audio playback cannot be assumed. The modality principle's preference for narration on transient visuals now collides with a delivery constraint: if audio may not play, words offloaded to speech can simply vanish. The transfer test is whether you can keep the dual-channel reasoning while treating the transcript or on-screen text as the primary, not the fallback, channel, perhaps converting an animation to a stepped, learner-paced sequence of static frames so the words can be read. If your modality skill assumes headphones and stable streaming, it does not transfer; the durable skill is choosing channels against the actual delivery conditions, not the ideal ones.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/03-04-narration-and-modality-decisions-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"03.04\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes for AIL 606 03.04. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_narration_and_modality_decisions\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace links Narration and Modality Decisions, evidence source, support choice, and revision quality. That is what separates design learning from simple completion.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nSee how narration and modality decisions tie back to evidence-based revision and bounded AI support across the dual-channel choice.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_multimedia_storyboarding_and_ai_asset_generation\",\"ail606_supplement_narration_and_modality_decisions\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_4_narration_and_modality_decisions_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_narration_and_modality_decisions","stem":"A frame shows an animation of cell division while the narrator reads, word for word, the full paragraph of text already printed on the same screen. Which principle does this violate?","options":["The personalization principle, because the tone is too formal.","The redundancy principle, because identical on-screen text and narration force the learner to process the same words in two channels.","The pre-training principle, because key terms were not defined.","No principle; reading the text aloud always helps."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Verbatim on-screen text plus narration is redundant and splits the visual channel between the animation and the text. Narrate over the visual, or show the text, but not both saying the same words.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_3_4_read_screen_aloud","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_narration_and_modality_decisions"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_4_narration_and_modality_decisions_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_narration_and_modality_decisions","stem":"A designer converts a multi-row reference table of conversion factors into audio-only narration, citing the modality principle. Why is this a misapplication?","options":["Audio is always lower quality than text.","Reference content that learners must inspect and re-check belongs as durable text; transient audio forces re-listening and overloads memory.","The modality principle applies only to children.","Tables can never be narrated."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The modality benefit assumes transient, learner-paced explanatory content alongside a graphic. Reference or complex material that must be re-inspected is better as text the learner can scan.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_3_4_narration_always_better","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_narration_and_modality_decisions"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_4_narration_and_modality_decisions_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_narration_and_modality_decisions","stem":"An AI tool offers to auto-add narration to a frame that already has dense on-screen text over a busy diagram. What design judgment must you apply before accepting?","options":["Accept it, since more channels always means more learning.","Recognize that modality offloads words to audio so the eyes can read the graphic; stacking text, audio, and a busy image overloads rather than offloads.","Accept it only if the narrator voice is female.","Reject all AI narration on principle."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The modality principle works by moving words off the crowded visual channel. Keeping the text and adding narration on top of a busy diagram defeats the purpose and overloads both channels.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_3_4_text_with_picture_is_modality","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_narration_and_modality_decisions"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_4_narration_and_modality_decisions_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_narration_and_modality_decisions","stem":"You decide narration over animation is the right modality for a process frame. To make the decision defensible across your learner population, what must accompany the narration?","options":["A louder default volume.","A synchronized caption or transcript, so learners who are deaf, hard of hearing, or in audio-off contexts still get the content.","A faster playback option only.","Nothing; narration alone is sufficient."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A modality choice is only defensible when it includes an equivalent. Captions or a transcript keep the audio channel from gating access for part of the audience.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_3_4_ignore_accessibility_in_modality","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_narration_and_modality_decisions"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_4_narration_and_modality_decisions_adaptive_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"evidence_based_revision","stem":"A learner leaves a 'confusion' annotation with low confidence on a frame that swapped narration for printed text. Their revision changed only the font and text size. Which combination of signals should drive the next adaptive move?","options":["The completion status: the frame was finished, so advance to the next section.","The note type plus the revision quality: a confusion annotation with low confidence and a cosmetic-only revision shows the modality logic was never addressed, so the system should explain or re-represent the principle.","The font choice: better typography would have resolved the confusion.","The confidence score alone: low confidence always means more practice items."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Adaptive support reads the combination of the annotation type, the artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality. Here the confusion note plus a polish-only revision (font and size, not the narration-versus-text decision) signals the learner has not engaged the modality principle itself, so the right move targets the reasoning, not the styling.","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_4_read_screen_aloud","pattern":"redundant_onscreen_text_and_narration","description":"Narrating the exact same words shown on screen, violating the redundancy principle and splitting the visual channel","trigger":"learner pairs a full text paragraph with narration that reads it verbatim","feedback":"Do not narrate identical on-screen text word for word. Either narrate over a visual or show the text, not both saying the same thing.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_4_narration_always_better","pattern":"modality_principle_overgeneralized","description":"Believing narration is always superior to text, ignoring that the modality benefit depends on transient content, learner control, and context","trigger":"learner converts a reference table or complex equation to audio-only narration","feedback":"The modality principle has limits. Reference, complex, or learner-paced content often belongs as inspectable text, not transient audio.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_4_text_with_picture_is_modality","pattern":"modality_confused_with_adding_media","description":"Thinking modality means adding more media channels rather than offloading words from the overloaded visual channel to the audio channel","trigger":"learner adds narration on top of dense on-screen text and a busy diagram","feedback":"Modality offloads words to audio so the eyes can process the graphic. Stacking text plus audio plus a busy image overloads, not offloads.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_4_ignore_accessibility_in_modality","pattern":"audio_only_excludes_learners","description":"Choosing audio-only narration without a text equivalent, excluding deaf or hard-of-hearing learners and those without audio","trigger":"learner ships narration with no captions or transcript","feedback":"A modality choice must include an equivalent. Provide captions or a transcript so the audio channel does not gate access.","rail_action":"practice"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"d667c56e4c84ff56d579f62437ff29f1345784b1b0330e75ea4e588bc9c24bbe","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}